From 3eb41d5f2e605fe1995bbb3e105e2ac28f9a8028 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oleg Eterevsky <oleg@eterevsky.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 10:29:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Donate a slice update's operand through the stream's own
 command-buffer pins

`SliceUpdate` and `DynamicSliceUpdate` copy their whole operand into the
output before writing the window unless the operand is donatable.  On the
Metal backend an operand that any kernel of a still-live command buffer
read is never donatable: `gpu::eval` keeps a `shared_ptr<Data>` on every
input of every primitive it encodes until the buffer completes, so
`array::is_donatable()` -- a use-count test -- sees a second holder.  For
a decode loop's KV cache (read by the layer's attention, then written by
the next layer's update, then read by the next token's attention) that
is every update: an 8 KiB write costs a 20 MiB copy, every time.

Those pins keep the buffer ALIVE while the GPU may still read it; on the
stream that encoded the reads they are not an observer of its contents,
because a kernel encoded later on the same stream is ordered after them:
within an encoder by the write-after-read barrier `register_output_array`
already inserts, across encoders by a fence.  This patch lets the two
slice updates donate through such pins, and makes that ordering explicit:

* `array::Data` gains a 64-bit pin word (count, count from the open
  encoder generation, the generation, the stream), maintained by
  compare-and-swap from `detail::pin_data` / `unpin_data`.
* `array::is_donatable(bool through_stream_pins)`: with the flag, a
  buffer whose extra holders are exactly the pins of the calling
  stream is donatable; the strict `is_donatable()` is unchanged, and so
  is every primitive that calls it.  Pins from another stream, or from
  more encoders than the word counts, keep the strict answer.
* `gpu::eval` sets a per-thread eval context (stream, encoder generation)
  around `eval_gpu`, and pins through the encoder (`CommandEncoder::pin`,
  one pin per encoder per buffer, one completion handler per command
  buffer instead of one per primitive).  The handler releases the pins
  BEFORE the scheduler's and the dispatch accounting's completion
  notices.
* A donation that saw through pins of encoders that have already ended
  marks the encoder, and `end_encoding` then waits on the fence of every
  encoder still in flight -- fence waits apply to the whole compute pass,
  so the pass runs after everything committed ahead of it.  Paid only by
  an encoder that donated that way.
* `SliceUpdate::eval_gpu` (Metal) and `DynamicSliceUpdate::eval_gpu`
  (gpu) donate a row-contiguous, same-dtype operand through the pins.
  `metal::dispatch_stats()` reports how many did and how many copied.

The CUDA backend never sets the eval context, so `is_donatable(true)` is
the strict test there and nothing changes.
